1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A directed graph is a set of one-way streets. Reachable is easy; the interesting question is whether you can get back.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,000
If the return trip exists, the two vertices are mutually reachable, and they are in the same strongly connected component.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,000
A single vertex is always its own component, even with no edges at all, because a vertex reaches itself by the empty path.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
So the question is not about edges. It is about a whole set of vertices that can all reach each other, in both directions, every pair.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Write u ~ v when there is a path from u to v and a path from v to u. The empty path counts, so u ~ u always.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
The relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive, so it is an equivalence relation, and its classes are the components.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Two consequences, both used in every question: the components are disjoint, and they cover every vertex exactly once.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
A graph is strongly connected exactly when it has one component, which is why the phrase is "component", not "strongly connected set".

9
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Nine vertices, thirteen edges. The components are A B C, D E, F G, and H I: four of them.

10
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Within a component every pair reaches every other. Between components the traffic runs one way: C reaches D, but nothing returns.

11
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:22,000
The count is 4. Checking it by hand means, for each vertex, finding everything it reaches and everything that reaches it, and intersecting.

12
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Two traversals are enough. Kosaraju needs two passes over the graph; Tarjan needs one. Both are linear in vertices plus edges.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Pass one is an ordinary depth-first search of the graph, started at E, then A, then F. Every vertex gets a discovery and a finish time.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:46,000
From E the search goes to D, which finishes at 3; then E walks to F, G, H, I and finishes at 12. A finishes last, at 18.

15
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Push each vertex on a stack as it finishes. The stack reads D I H G F E C B A: the order the search finished, bottom to top.

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Pass one proves nothing on its own. It produces one list, and the second pass consumes that list from the top down.

17
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:14,000
The vertex with the largest finish time, here A, lies in a source component: one with no edge coming into it from another component.

18
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:22,000
The reason: if component X has an edge into component Y, the last vertex of X finishes after the last vertex of Y.

19
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:30,000
So ordering the components by their largest finish time gives a topological order of the condensation, largest first.

20
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
That is why the list from pass one is worth keeping: it is the condensation in topological order, written in vertices.

21
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Pass two reverses every edge, and runs the same search on the reversed graph, taking the vertices in decreasing finish order.

22
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:54,000
The first tree is rooted at A and reaches only A B C. In the reversed graph, nothing outside that component leads back in.

23
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:02,000
The next unvisited vertex in the order is E, then F, then H. Four trees, four components: A B C, D E, F G, H I.

24
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Two passes, each linear. Every tree of the second pass is one component, and every component is exactly one tree.

25
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Both details are load-bearing. Run pass two on the original graph and the answer is wrong on a graph with two vertices and one edge.

26
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Take the vertices in ascending finish order instead and the same two vertices break it again: B first, then A, and one tree covers both.

27
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:36,000
With both details right, the first tree cannot escape: an edge leaving the tree in the transpose would be an edge entering the component in the original.

28
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
So the recipe is: search, stack on finish, transpose, search again in stack order, and each tree is a component.

29
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Contract each component to a single vertex. Nine vertices become four, and thirteen edges become four distinct ones.

30
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:56,000
The result has no cycle, always. If it had one, those components would all be mutually reachable and would have been one component.

31
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:04,000
The condensation is the graph that survives: it is a DAG, it has a topological order, and it is where source and sink questions live.

32
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Two counts worth knowing: the number of components, and the number of distinct edges between them. Both are read off the condensation.

33
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Tarjan finds the same partition in one pass, by giving each vertex a second number: the low-link value.

34
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:28,000
On the drawn graph the values come out 0 0 0 for the first component, 3 3, 5 5, 7 7. Equal within a component, larger across components.

35
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:36,000
When a vertex finds low equal to its own index, everything on the stack above it is one component, and it is popped.

36
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
One pass instead of two, no transpose, same answer. The two algorithms agree on every graph this page can enumerate.

37
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:50,000
A standard question: how many edges must be added to make the whole graph strongly connected? Add one here, from the sink back to the source.

38
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:58,000
On the condensation: count the sources, the sinks, and the isolated components. The answer is the larger of the first two, plus the isolated count.

39
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Dropping the isolated term is wrong: one edge A to B plus an isolated C needs two edges, not the one the shortcut gives.

40
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Two boundaries: a one-component graph needs nothing, and a condensation with two isolated components needs two edges.

41
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:20,000
GATE CSE 2014, Set 1: which of four derived graphs has the same strongly connected components as G?

42
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:28,000
The transpose. Reversing every edge reverses every path, so u reaches v becomes v reaches u, and mutual reachability is untouched.

43
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:34,000
The page reports honestly that the length-two option also preserved the components in its search, so the item is best read as asking for the definitional answer.

44
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:40,000
Five steps: define mutual reachability, contract, read the condensation, then answer the count, the order, or the augmentation.

